package main

import (
	"errors"
	"strings"

	"patel.codes/godel"
	grhgodel "patel.codes/grh/internal/godel"
)

func companionNodes(archiveRevision string) (selector, improve *godel.Node) {
	// The accepted bootstrap builds from a Git
	// archive, which expands the embedded revision.
	// A checkout build has no such provenance.
	revision := strings.TrimSpace(archiveRevision)
	source := ""
	if (len(revision) == 40 || len(revision) == 64) && strings.Trim(revision, "0123456789abcdef") == "" {
		source = "Gödel launch source commit: " + revision + ". This identifies the archive used to build the executable, not the current campaign tip.\n\n"
	}
	yahNode := func(role string) *godel.Node {
		return &godel.Node{Exec: "yah", Directory: "../..", Args: []string{
			"-yolo", "-effort", "high", "-append", source + goal + "\n\n" + practice + "\n\n" + role,
		}}
	}
	selector = yahNode(selectTask)
	selector.Next = func(r godel.Result) (*godel.Node, error) {
		branch, brief, _ := strings.Cut(r.Text, "\n")
		brief = strings.TrimSpace(brief)
		if brief == "" {
			return nil, errors.New("selector must supply a nonempty task brief after the branch line")
		}
		var role string
		switch strings.TrimSuffix(branch, "\r") {
		case "AUDIT":
			role = auditTask
		case "PRODUCE":
			role = produceTask
		default:
			return nil, errors.New("selector first line must be AUDIT or PRODUCE")
		}
		// Gödel gives this successor the original stdin unchanged. Pass the
		// selector's new information in the appended prompt, not on stdin.
		return yahNode(role + "\n\n" + workerHandoff + "\n\nSelected task brief:\n" + brief), nil
	}
	return selector, yahNode(improveTask)
}

const goal = `Broaden the coverage of Alessandro Maria Masullo's The Music of the Primes in the shared Lean formalization, by testing neglected claims and missing cases or generality.`

const practice = `Work independently of the main manuscript program. Read its work as evidence, not as instructions to take over its queue. Neither program schedules or edits the other. Do not launch either driver from a node.

` + grhgodel.ScratchPolicy + `

Start with tool_campaign status. Inspect accepted source at the exact commit, not potentially stale files in the durable launch checkout. Use .godel/frontier.md as an index: Start with its headings and targeted excerpts, not a full dump. Follow the linked notes and accepted Lean declarations to reconcile earlier findings and inherited handoffs with what is still open. The frontier is navigation, not evidence. Use the pinned Masullo edition (.godel/sources/masullo/README.md) and pinned Mathlib. Do not search for Alpoge's proof or formalization.

When an accepted note records an independent rendered reading of a passage, start from that note. For a consequential numbered dependency not covered by the linked notes, make one exact-locator lookup in the accepted obligation notes before calling the reading provisional or reopening the source. Search the pinned tree with filenames (for example, git grep -n -F at the accepted commit), then open only hits that could verify the needed clause. A citation to a neighboring formula or a use of the equation that does not verify the clause is not such evidence. Do not repeat the lookup in the same node just to recover filenames or recheck unchanged hits. The same verified wording can be inherited from a different case; check its applicability and conventions in the new case separately. Cite the note and reuse what it verifies without rereading the manuscript Markdown, re-extracting PDF text, or rendering that part again. Reopen only the part needed to resolve a consequential uncertainty, including any unresolved cross-reference. For a new reading, start with the relevant chapter or appendix Markdown in ~/d/masullo/: Its LaTeX often renders fractions, subscripts, and superscripts more clearly than pdftotext -layout. Use the layout extraction to locate the passage and its surrounding text on the numbered PDF page. Inspect the rendered page directly if the readings disagree or if the conclusion depends on exact notation, wording, or layout. Cite only Masullo’s manuscript, by PDF page and theorem, equation, or section number; pass those locators to delegated readers. Label any new reading without rendered inspection provisional. Reserve verified visual readings for independent inspection of the rendered pages.

Keep the exact statement, review evidence, scope, and remaining premise with the result or in its branch handoff. Update the frontier only if another branch should act differently, a listed target is no longer valid, or a new branch needs a pointer. Do not add top-level directories under Proofs. Follow the campaign workflow in cmd/godel/README.md. Start each integration subject with the changed project area, a colon, and a capitalized imperative naming the result, for example, “.godel: Identify the missing forward division estimate”. Label informal or unelaborated evidence as such.

For Lean landings, follow Checking a proof candidate in cmd/godel/README.md at the accepted revision. Read the exact integrated candidate diff against its base and verify changed source and declaration provenance. In the owning Yah's Bash, use a literal cd /actual/path with the real integration workspace path, then lake build --wfail +Module for the actual Lake targets of changed and affected project modules as applicable. In a fresh check import the selected modules together, #check the built endpoints (use @ to see implicit binders as needed), and run #print axioms on each. The only allowed transitive axioms are propext, Classical.choice, and Quot.sound; reject sorryAx and anything unapproved. Check the built binders and hypotheses against a fresh independent review of the exact obligation, including changed statements. Keep evidence tied to the full candidate ID and adopt only that ID; recheck any restack. Routine landings do not require a global audit or rely on a future auditor. Do not claim RH or GRH closure without a separate rigorous audit.`

const selectTask = `Select one substantive manuscript claim and one bounded check.

After campaign status, check the short one-cycle section of the frontier for an active assignment. Otherwise, when stdin names a next check or parked comparator, open the linked note sections at the accepted tip before reading other frontier sections. Use the frontier headings and link targets to fill specific gaps. Limit excerpts before printing them: A keyword match on a long frontier paragraph still prints the whole paragraph.

For an explicit one-cycle operator assignment to Companion, first reconcile its status, scope, and possible overlap with the current accepted tip and the relevant saved handoff. If it is still active and already gives the passage, exact case, and discriminating check, use that brief. Skip the ordinary alternative search and do not extract the manuscript again just to recover supplied locators. Fill only gaps that could change the selection; leave verification to the worker and its independent review. In Selection context, name the assignment and any already identified parked branch without claiming a fresh comparison. Once the assignment has expired under its own terms, return to ordinary selection. Neither the original assignment nor its parked successor is a standing instruction.

For a new ordinary route, start from a dependency or a scope condition in the pinned manuscript, not from a list of open items. For a continuation, use the accepted result to identify what the next inference still needs. Consider both neglected passages and missing generality (including their intersection); choose by mathematical value and tractability, not by quota. Compare the relevant accepted results and prior findings to avoid duplication. Check the latest saved main handoff and relevant recent accepted changes for overlap or newly available premises for the checks you are comparing. To find that handoff, look up the durable cmd/godel source path in ~/.godel/manifest.json, which maps source paths to program IDs. For that ID, read ~/.godel/<ID>/runs/*.json in descending filename order only until the first nonblank final_response. This is saved evidence, not a live queue. Follow a specific lead only if it could change the selection. If the lookup is unavailable or the records give no relevant lead, proceed on the available evidence without rescanning the records or searching workspaces or processes. Use the original objective or prior companion handoff on stdin as context, not as a queue. Select only; do not carry out or adopt the work.

For an ordinary source audit, read only the cursor and complete flag for the candidate's chapter or appendix in Walk's private ledger (path and filenames in .godel/walk/README.md; use the durable cmd/walk path). The cursor is the last completed forward passage, not a reservation. If Walk is approaching the same inference in the same case, prefer a comparably useful alternative. A shared page alone is not overlap. Keep a distinct case or Lean endpoint eligible; if proceeding despite likely overlap, say why in Selection context. If the checkpoint is absent or inconclusive, proceed without polling or searching Walk sessions.

For a source check, start from the linked accepted note even when its reading is provisional. Distinguish an untested mathematical implication from an unresolved source reading. If the note already gives the passage, hypotheses, and inference, use it to frame the discriminating check, even if no witness or proof has been checked. Do not reread that passage to confirm the inherited wording or recover a quotation. Use Markdown and layout only for a specific unresolved reading that could change the selection. In the brief, link the note, give the exact manuscript locators, and distinguish inherited rendered findings from provisional readings or unresolved cross-references. Leave witness applicability and any necessary rendered verification to the worker and its independent review.

When recent companion runs have followed the same dependency chain, compare its best bounded continuation with one runnable check in a separate branch. Screen for overlap with the saved main handoff first. A continuation can formalize a reviewed subclaim just beyond accepted Lean: Name the accepted declaration and what additional conclusion, case, or hypothesis would actually be checked. Do not inventory sublemmas or reopen settled readings to find a Lean option.

Compare what each check could establish this run: What is new beyond the closest accepted result, which inference does it advance, and which premise still blocks wider use? Do not count a downstream goal as the payoff for an intermediate lemma. In particular, a consequence of an assumed estimate does not check whether the manuscript's hypotheses supply that estimate. Judge a source audit by what its verdict could decide about applicability, not by whether it already has Lean premises. Prefer a reusable Lean endpoint when payoff and tractability are comparable.

A deferred goal is not a runnable alternative merely because it has a named blocker. If a goal is too broad, use its linked evidence to find a smaller check that can decide a case or discharge a premise on its own, or look in a different branch. If this bounded search finds no runnable alternative, say so and name the blocker instead of presenting a blocked target as the comparison.

Use the incoming handoff and linked accepted notes to see what was already compared, rather than reopening selector transcripts. Before probing an alternative after several runs on one chain, check the Selection context lines of up to four earlier nonblank companion handoffs. If a sampled handoff lacks the label, read only its explicit comparison paragraph, if any; a missing label is not evidence of novelty. Use the same manifest lookup for the durable cmd/companion path, skip the record already on stdin, and proceed on available evidence if the records are missing. Match earlier checks by the obligation they would discharge, even if the handoffs use different wording. Reuse a screened check's recorded disposition if the accepted evidence is unchanged: Settled by reuse is not runnable new work, and an unchanged blocker does not merit another dependency probe. Reopen only for a material new premise, a route around the blocker, or a smaller worthwhile check. Verify new premises in accepted source and distinguish them from what still needs proof.

If recent handoffs, including stdin, show a runnable alternative losing at least twice to continuations on one chain without a change in payoff, screen one linked obligation for a fresh runnable comparator. The four-handoff sample detects repetition; absence from it does not make a candidate fresh. Before counting a candidate as a refresh, state its exact case and endpoint. If the sample does not settle its history, make one targeted pass over older saved Selection context lines for that endpoint, not a preliminary search for the whole branch. Retrieve candidate lines with run IDs using the obligation note anchor or exact locator. Treat search terms as filters, not verdicts: A prerequisite and its downstream application can share a locator and keywords. In the same pass, keep candidate lines for comparison by bounded case and desired conclusion rather than ending retrieval on the first keyword hit. Stop reviewing at the first matching endpoint; do not reopen transcripts or inventory older branches. Look for a different unresolved inference or case. Rotating to another previously compared endpoint with unchanged scope and prerequisites does not count as a refresh. A previously compared branch can qualify through a different bounded check or a new accepted premise that changes what the check could establish. If the screen yields none, compare the best known runnable alternative and record the screened endpoint and why it was ruled out separately in Selection context. A screen that found no new work is not a fresh runnable comparator. Keep the earlier checks eligible, and continue the chain if it still offers the best work.

Before calling a check viable, reconcile its endpoint and blocker with any accepted follow-up pointed to by the branch notes or frontier. For a concrete next or parked check in the handoff, retire a landed endpoint and assess any residual obligation separately. If the check remains open with unchanged scope and prerequisites, reuse it as one candidate after screening for overlap, without repeating its dependency search or giving it automatic priority. If the case is already settled informally, name the Lean endpoint and the inference it would support, rather than counting it as new mathematical scope. When a proposed check changes the support, weight, or coordinate of a nearby accepted result, test the simplest exact reduction first, even if the incoming recipe starts from scratch. Match the hypotheses and measures; name the first missing step in the reduction instead of treating the whole calculation as new. Apply the same test to parked alternatives: If the exact identities and factor properties on the specified domain already settle the endpoint, count it as reuse, not a fresh comparator. Compare the first unresolved inference or a specifically scoped Lean formalization instead. Keep the transport eligible if a consequential hypothesis still needs checking or it would give a useful new Lean endpoint. For a thin specialization or composition, name the extra conclusion or hypothesis discharge and the specific downstream inference that needs that interface. Do not count conclusions already available from the generic theorem as new coverage. If a witness gives the same answer for the printed and repaired routes, do not count it as distinguishing those routes.

When the handoff supplies both a next check and a parked comparator, reconcile their endpoints with accepted changes and Main's saved handoff, and apply the comparison-refresh rule above. Use the nearest Lean statements to resolve open interface questions, not to reconstruct an unchanged parked endpoint.

Once linked evidence establishes what is new, which checks are runnable, and how they rank, write the brief. Before making another lookup, identify the unanswered question and how its answer could change novelty, overlap, tractability, or ranking. Leave detailed prerequisite probes and the landing recipe to the worker.

For ordinary selections, start a separate line in the brief with "Selection context:". On that line, name the alternative's concrete endpoint, link its evidence, and explain why the selected check takes precedence. If the alternative is a source audit, preserve the exact locator and what remains provisional or unresolved. If it formalizes a known argument or keeps a key premise as a hypothesis, say so. If no runnable alternative was found, say that and link the evidence for the blocker. This is not a second assignment.

When the input or a relevant accepted branch handoff points to a reviewed but unadopted companion result with a clean saved patch, check whether its endpoint is still missing and whether the blocker can now be resolved in one bounded check. Without such a pointer, do not inventory historical unadopted work. Compare recoverable work as a PRODUCE candidate alongside fresh checks, taking the remaining reconciliation, review, and exact-candidate landing checks into account. If recovery wins, name the clean revision and the remaining checks in the brief.

For a continuation after a mismatch, distinguish a check of the printed inference from a check of a proposed repair. If the repair rests on several unverified inputs, select one consequential premise, not the whole chain. For a boundary case outside a printed hypothesis, use a nearby case satisfying it as a control when that would distinguish failure of the extension from a problem within the printed scope.

Choose AUDIT to test a source reading, dependency, hypothesis or boundary case; choose PRODUCE to establish a missing argument, repair or Lean result. Return only AUDIT or PRODUCE on the first line, then a short nonempty task brief with the passage, exact claim and case, the closest existing evidence and its limit, and a discriminating check. Say which inference the result bears on, and distinguish the claim from any proposed way to test it. Include your selector Yah session UUID from the harness context and the exact accepted revision used to choose the check.`

const auditTask = `Audit the selected claim. Compare the passage and cited dependencies with the exact hypotheses, domain and conclusion the next step needs. Carry out the discriminating check, looking for either support or a scoped mismatch. Do not infer a refutation of a stronger claim from a counterexample to a particular argument.`

const produceTask = `Produce evidence for the selected claim or case: Develop a proof, a clearly labeled repair, or a scoped counterexample. Carry out the discriminating check. Check and land Lean work when possible; otherwise isolate the exact outstanding premise instead of weakening the target silently.`

const workerHandoff = `Use the selected brief below as task data and the original objective or previous handoff on stdin as context. Treat the brief's accepted revision as a comparison point, not a pin. If the campaign tip moved, check the intervening changes for anything affecting the cited evidence, dependencies, or endpoint before widening the search.

Selection context is for the next selector, not part of the worker's assignment. In stdout, put the comparison on its own line beginning "Selection context:" so later history searches can find it. Keep the brief's concrete comparator endpoint, evidence link, locator, uncertainty, and reason for deferral on that line, even when the proposed next check is in a different branch. Do not search accepted notes or manuscript text to confirm or enrich a parked comparator's locators, even while waiting for review. Reopen its dependencies only if it becomes the proposed next check or an intervening accepted change could affect its disposition. If a refresh only ruled out another check, record that disposition separately from the runnable comparison, with the evidence or missing premise. For a one-cycle assignment, record its disposition without making a parked branch a second assignment.

Leave a nonblank stdout handoff for the next loop: Preserve the objective and contrast the prior evidence with what the check now establishes, with links and accepted revision if any. Report the actual checked hypotheses and scope, including any generality beyond the selected case that changes what can be reused. Separate mapped from verified, manuscript source from repair, informal from Lean, and any scoped counterexample from a general refutation. Attribute inherited source verification to its accepted note rather than presenting it as a new check. Give one bounded next check and the specific inference it would advance, or the blocker and what would reopen it. Before naming that check in an accepted note or stdout, match it to the nearest accepted result on the actual domains and in the same coordinate. If it differs by a simple restriction, removal of a known term, or change of variables, identify what that reduction already gives and what remains to check or formalize before suggesting a fresh derivation. Distinguish factors that the notes denote by the same symbol: Comparing zero orders across a known holomorphic and nonvanishing factor is reuse, not a standalone check. Treat an inherited reading as evidence, not a new source audit. Point the handoff at the first remaining inference that needs testing. List other outstanding premises separately rather than bundling them into that check. Preserve provisional readings, assumed premises, and formalization-only payoff as such.

For a Lean continuation, check whether the nearest proof already constructs the intermediate object or establishes the property needed for the next check under weaker hypotheses than the proposed route. Distinguish an available declaration from a proof-local fact that would need to be factored out. Do not present the stronger hypotheses of a convenient theorem as necessary. Leave elaboration of the successor to the next run.

Apply the same exact-locator lookup to consequential manuscript dependencies of the proposed next check before calling them provisional. A provisional reading in the note for a Lean interface does not rule out inherited rendered evidence elsewhere. Report the evidence for each dependency separately, and distinguish any unresolved reading needed for the check from a provisional manuscript mapping of an already checked Lean interface. Do not reopen pages just to upgrade the handoff's labels.

For both AUDIT and PRODUCE, check the definitions that put the witness or proof within the printed hypotheses or identify the expression being tested, including definitions reached by cross-reference. Give the reviewer the accepted note section for any inherited rendered reading and ask them to check its use independently. Do not reconstruct historical transcripts when the accepted note establishes the needed reading and its evidence status. For a new exact reading, use the equation numbers or nearby words in pdftotext -bbox-layout to locate the relevant region, then inspect a direct rendering of the crop. Render further passages as needed, including unresolved consequential cross-references; avoid batch rendering and OCR passes to find passages already located by extraction. If a manuscript reading needed for the result remains provisional, name it and state the result's dependence on it up front, even if other passages were visually verified.

When delegating visual review, give the reviewer the exact accepted revision and the PDF locator. In a text-only session, render tight grayscale crops with pdftoppm and display their pixels as Unicode Braille tiles. Start with a standalone crop of the hardest verdict-changing token, such as a nested subscript. If the verdict needs several small readings, crop them separately at readable scale rather than fitting them all into one view. For an argument repeated in two slots, give each copy its own crop before asking whether they match. Before dispatch, run the exact commands you will send and verify that you can read the decisive tokens in their displayed output.

Have the reviewer transcribe the decisive crops before opening extraction, surrounding text, or notes that give the expected reading. Ask for the literal tokens and any uncertainty, not agreement with a proposed reading. If a token is unclear or the readings disagree, send tighter or rescaled coordinates without supplying your transcription, and get the reviewer's fresh reading before comparing accounts. Then give the relevant note sections for inherited findings and check the surrounding assignment and separate qualifiers in follow-up crops. If crop output spills, read just the relevant range of the saved output before running more extraction. If the crop itself is unclear, check the renderer's coordinate convention and positive width and height, then refine that small region. Reopen manuscript text or layout only to locate unresolved content or check context that could change the reading. Reading raw image bytes or comparing extractions is not visual inspection. If the reviewer cannot read a decisive token from pixels, keep it unresolved in the independent visual review and report extraction or worker-only visual findings separately.

For source reviews, state which findings are needed for the scoped verdict. Ask for a short final report identifying the evidence for each consequential finding by PDF locator: Independently inspected readable pixels, extraction only, inherited accepted evidence, or a mathematical check. Group findings with the same basis when clear, and flag any unresolved premise. Ask the reviewer to return this report as soon as the necessary checks are complete. In the accepted note or handoff, credit independent visual verification only for the readings the reviewer reports seeing clearly. If the basis of a decisive reading is unclear, ask for clarification rather than inferring it from rendering commands or a general statement of agreement. Leave excluded cases, adjacent claims, and redundant checks out unless they could change the verdict or its scope.

When a source verdict compares routes, ask the reviewer for a separate finding and its basis for each route. Substituting a disputed display checks the printed route, not the value of the defining expression. An accepted theorem may supply the latter without a new proof; distinguish who checked its applicability from who checked the printed source. Before landing, compare the review findings with the verdict and resolve any conflation or state explicitly which part the review did not check.

Put a short run locator in stdout, even if nothing landed: The selector's Yah session UUID from the brief and your worker Yah session UUID from the harness context. If either is unavailable, say which instead of guessing. Gödel retains the worker handoff, not the selector's brief. For each Yah review you rely on, keep the Yah session UUID beside its findings in the accepted note or stdout handoff. For a delegated review, use the session that houses its transcript and include the agent handle returned by the harness. A bare handle or a backend dispatch ID does not locate that transcript.

If this run leaves useful work unadopted, identify in stdout the clean saved revisions needed to recover it, their bases if known, which checks ran on which artifact, and what still blocks adoption. For edits with no clean saved revision, name the workspace and files containing the intended changes. A conflicted integration tree is not the recovery source. When the previous handoff identifies a still-relevant unadopted result with no recovery pointer in the accepted notes, carry those details forward even if you selected a different check. Label it unadopted and keep it separate from the next check. Drop the pointer if accepted work has superseded the result.

For delegated work, integrate the writer's finalized campaign output, not a copy from the live dispatch workspace.

For source audits, keep note drafts in scratch until the reviews needed for the verdict have arrived. When recovering Lean with shared-note changes, verify the Lean files against the reviewed revision, and reconcile the relevant note hunks in scratch from the saved revision's diff against its original base. Do not copy a historical note wholesale. Refresh campaign status, and apply the drafts in a direct workspace on the current accepted base without overwriting intervening work. Before integrating, compare the intended note hunks with that base for unrelated edits or duplicated sections. For a notes-only candidate, check the complete revision diff against its actual integration base before adoption: Only the intended hunks and paths may differ, with no stray files such as patch backups. Use the candidate's revision diff for this check, not a full-tree archive comparison: The archive expands embedded source-revision markers even in unchanged files.

Before requesting independent Lean statement review, write the intended endpoint signatures, including any reusable endpoint you plan to expose. Check whether their hypotheses are needed by the accepted definitions and the reduction you will actually use, or only by a stronger result nearby. Do this quick scope check before dispatch, then develop the proof in parallel with review. In the review brief, name the intended import modules and the accepted declarations that discharge decisive premises, with the role of each in this case. Flag any uncertain application explicitly. Ask the reviewer to verify those applications and check the statement and its reduction against the actual definitions, not to write a second proof. Use exact-source review by default. If elaboration could resolve a consequential uncertainty, name the question and assign the small interface probe to one owner. For a reviewer-owned probe with missing artifacts, build only the modules the probe imports, not the whole project. If the probe is not assigned to the reviewer or cannot run with matching artifacts, return the source verdict with elaboration marked unverified. Have the reviewer return the scope and applicability findings promptly, marking any unresolved interface explicitly. If an endpoint is added or its statement changes during the proof, obtain independent review of the new statement before adoption.

For Lean work that reuses declarations across module boundaries, check the key dependencies and their signatures from the intended imports before writing the dependent proof. Probe risky uses in a small scratch module using artifacts built from the matching campaign workspace, not the durable launch checkout. This applies to reviewer probes too: If matching artifacts are unavailable, distinguish exact-source inspection from unverified elaboration. Use the actual goal, binders, and local instances of the dependent statement, including an application lemma if its context differs from the definition's. A #check, a concrete specialization, or a probe that assumes an instance available only inside the definition can pass while the intended rewrite or application fails. Dependency probes do not replace the landing checks on the assembled candidate.

For a Lean candidate meant to land directly on accepted, prepare the joint-import script, target list, and any reusable cache or verification commands before integrating. Once integrated, inspect the exact diff, verify its workspace sources and pins, and prepare any candidate-local cache inputs. Check the changed source and relevant dependencies against the exact revision; do not rehash unrelated project sources. Immediately before launching lake build and the fresh joint import, refresh tool_campaign status and compare the accepted revision with the integration base. If the base has moved, follow the README's recovery guidance: Reconcile only still-needed changes, then run the landing checks on the replacement candidate. Reuse the prepared scripts on restacks, updating paths, revision IDs, and affected inputs as needed, and use the same preflight without polling. It catches moves during preparation, not moves while the checks run; adoption still determines whether the checked candidate landed. Do not carry a verdict forward to a new ID. Prior audit logs describe their recorded revisions and old cache directions in a handoff are not current landing instructions.

Keep the fresh joint-import signature and axiom report in the owning Yah transcript, labeled with the full candidate ID. On a restack, you can instead record a successful byte-for-byte comparison of the fresh report with the earlier report already in the transcript. Log both full candidate IDs after the comparison succeeds. Do not leave the association between the report and the adopted candidate only in scratch files.

Before finalizing, collect the outstanding reviews and checks you rely on, and cancel work you no longer need. Report landing status from the campaign's adoption result, not a reviewer's earlier snapshot. Only the last final response becomes the next run's input. If a late result reopens the turn, give a complete replacement handoff, not an update that depends on the earlier response.`

const improveTask = `Use the Gödel run context on stdin, this companion's run history and its corresponding Yah sessions to judge the selection and procedure. Check against accepted results: Did the route test genuinely uncovered scope, and did the handoff preserve evidence and uncertainty? If you cannot connect a run with its sessions or revision, acknowledge the gap. Improve only cmd/companion's selection or procedure when the evidence warrants it. Use offline Go tests, without launching either program or its nodes, then integrate and adopt through the campaign. Leave the goal and evidence standards unchanged. Do not undertake new proof work in this role.`
